artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $(X,\Delta)$ be a projective klt pair (i.e., $X$ is a normal projective variety over $\mathbb{C}$, $\Delta$ is an effective $\mathbb{Q}$-divisor such that $K_X+\Delta$ is $\mathbb{Q}$-Cartier, and for any log resolution all discrepancies are strictly greater than $-1$). Assume that the anti-log-canonical divisor $-(K_X+\Delta)$ is nef. Do not assume that $X$ is smooth.

Establish a structure theorem for $(X,\Delta)$ analogous to the smooth case, namely: prove the existence of an everywhere-defined holomorphic maximally rationally connected (MRC) fibration $\varphi: X 	o Y$ onto a normal projective variety $Y$ with the following properties:

(1) $Y$ has numerically trivial canonical class (i.e., $K_Y \equiv 0$, in a suitable klt sense if $Y$ is singular);

(2) the pair $(X,\Delta)$ is locally trivial over $Y$ (i.e., locally analytically/étale over $Y$, $(X,\Delta)$ is a product of $(F,\Delta|_F)$ with a base, where $F$ is a general fiber);

(3) after passing to a suitable cover, a universal-cover product decomposition holds: the universal cover of $X$ (or of its smooth locus, suitably interpreted) decomposes as a product of the universal cover of $Y$ and the typical fiber $F$ of $\varphi$, in compatibility with a singular Beauville–Bogomolov decomposition of $Y$ into factors of Calabi–Yau and irreducible holomorphic symplectic type together with abelian factors.

Does such a structure theorem hold for arbitrary singular klt pairs $(X,\Delta)$ with $-(K_X+\Delta)$ nef?
